# Local development environment — COMMITTED, and deliberately not gitignored.
#
# This file exists so a local run has the same *shape* as production: a
# Postgres catalog reached over a URL, and audio addressed by object key and
# fetched over HTTP through a redirect. Only the endpoints differ.
#
#   production                          local (this file)
#   ------------------------------      --------------------------------
#   Supabase Postgres (pooler:6543)     PostgreSQL on 127.0.0.1:5433
#   Vercel Blob / Cloudflare R2         filesystem, served at /blobs/<key>
#   Jamendo API                         Jamendo API (same; needs a client id)
#
# PRECEDENCE: real environment variables > .env > .env.local. So this file is
# the shared default, `.env` is your personal/secret override, and an exported
# variable beats both. NOTHING SECRET GOES HERE — it is in git. Credentials
# belong in .env, which is gitignored. See .env.example.
#
# Bring the local services up with:  make up


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- database
# Local PostgreSQL, created and started by `make up`. Matches PGPORT/PGDB in
# the Makefile. The username is deliberately omitted: libpq then defaults to
# the OS user, which is what `initdb --auth=trust` set the superuser to, so
# this line is portable across machines instead of hardcoding one developer.
#
# This is the single most important line for "local reflects production":
# without it the backend silently runs on SQLite, and the dialect differences
# (JSONB vs TEXT, BOOLEAN vs INTEGER, pooling) go untested until deploy.
#
# In production this is Supabase's TRANSACTION POOLER on port 6543 — not the
# direct connection on 5432. See docs/database.md.
DJMIXER_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://127.0.0.1:5433/djmixer

# ------------------------------------------------------------ blob storage
# `local` stores objects on disk under DJMIXER_DATA and serves them from the
# app at /blobs/<key>. That is a real HTTP fetch through the same 302 the
# deployed app issues, using the same provider-agnostic keys
# (audio/<id>.wav, variants/<id>_<bpm>.wav, meta/<id>.json), so the client
# code path is identical. Set to `vercel` only when pointing at a real store.
BLOB_BACKEND=local

# Absolute base URL for object keys. LEFT UNSET ON PURPOSE for the local
# backend, which then emits same-origin paths like /blobs/audio/1001.wav.
# Setting it to an absolute origin would break the browser suite, which runs
# the app on a different port (5199) than a dev server (5050) — the redirect
# would point at whichever port was hardcoded here.
#
# In production this is the store's public origin, e.g.
#   https://<store-id>.public.blob.vercel-storage.com
# and switching to Cloudflare R2 means changing only this value.
# BLOB_BASE_URL=

# Where the local blob backend keeps its objects, and where SQLite lives when
# DJMIXER_DATABASE_URL is unset. Safe to delete; `make status` reports what is
# in it and the tiered `make clean-*` targets remove it.
DJMIXER_DATA=./data

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- ingestion
# NOT set here — it is a credential and this file is committed. Put it in .env:
#
#   JAMENDO_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id
#
# Without one, `mode: jamendo` ingestion fails with a message saying so, and
# `mode: offline` (tests/e2e/tracks.e2e.json) works with no credentials at all.

# ---------------------------------------------------------- what still differs
# Three things this cannot reproduce locally, listed so they are not mistaken
# for covered ground:
#
#   1. Supabase's transaction pooler. `make test-pg` runs against plain
#      PostgreSQL; prepare_threshold and connection-limit behaviour under the
#      pooler need a real Supabase instance.
#   2. Cross-origin audio delivery. Local blobs are same-origin, so the CORS
#      headers a real bucket needs are never exercised.
#   3. The Vercel function bundle. Nothing local enforces the 250 MB limit or
#      the read-only filesystem.
